      "provenance": {
        "notes": "Formerly Sansevieria cylindrica (not stored as a synonym: the name matcher then sends the plain input sansevieria here instead of to the snake plant); part of the snake-plant group (the snake plant of row 1 is Dracaena trifasciata). First entry whose base interval comes from a page: NC State says about every two weeks in summer and monthly in winter, so 14 days and a rest factor of 30/14 (about 2.1) are the agent's reading of that sentence, not a stated factor. RHS (genus guide) says water only once the compost starts to dry. Temperature: RHS genus guide best 15 to 24 C, keep above 10 C; NC State says bring indoors below 55 F (13 C); the RHS range is stored. Humidity: RHS says it dislikes high humidity, no figure. Size: the RHS species page gives 1 to 1.5 m by 0.1 to 0.5 m; NC State gives 6 in to 7 ft by 6 in to 2 ft. Habit: NC State erect from a basal rosette, mapped to upright (agent). Toxicity: NC State low severity, saponin, tagged a problem for cats, dogs and children, signs include dilated pupils, vomiting, drooling, weakness; the ASPCA entry is the genus Dracaena (toxic to dogs and cats, same signs). Graded 1 by the agent, as for the snake plant; RHS says harmful if eaten without detail. NC State says the sap irritates skin but contact dermatitis 'no', so no skin line."
      },
